" (https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~genkin/papers/vsql.pdf)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~genkin/papers/vsql.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2091\nL0@P0: vSQL: Verifying Arbitrary SQL Queries over\nL1@P0: Dynamic Outsourced Databases\nL2@P0: Yupeng Zhang∗, Daniel Genkin†,∗, Jonathan Katz∗, Dimitrios Papadopoulos‡,∗and Charalampos Papamanthou∗\nL3@P0: ∗University of Maryland †University of Pennsylvania ‡Hong Kong University of Science and Technology\nL4@P0: Email: {zhangyp,cpap}@umd.edu, danielg3@cis.upenn.edu, jkatz@cs.umd.edu, dipapado@cse.ust.hk\nL5@P0: Abstract—Cloud database systems such as Amazon RDS or\nL6@P0: Google Cloud SQL enable the outsourcing of a large database to\nL7@P0: a server who then responds to SQL queries. A natural problem\nL8@P0: here is to efficiently verify the correctness of responses returned\nL9@P0: by the (untrusted) server. In this paper we present vSQL, a\nL10@P0: novel cryptographic protocol for publicly verifiable SQL queries\nL11@P0: on dynamic databases. At a high level, our construction relies on\nL12@P0: two extensions of the CMT interactive-proof protocol [Cormode\nL13@P0: et al., 2012]: (i) supporting outsourced input via the use of a\nL14@P0: polynomial-delegation protocol with succinct proofs, and (ii) sup\u0002porting auxiliary input (i.e., non-deterministic computation) ef\u0002ficiently. Compared to previous verifiable-computation systems\nL15@P0: based on interactive proofs, our construction has verification cost\nL16@P0: polylogarithmic in the auxiliary input (which for SQL queries\nL17@P0: can be as large as the database) rather than linear.\nL18@P0: In order to evaluate the performance and expressiveness of\nL19@P0: our scheme, we tested it on SQL queries based on the TPC-H\nL20@P0: benchmark on a database with 6 × 106rows and 13 columns.\nL21@P0: The server overhead in our scheme (which is typically the\nL22@P0: main bottleneck) is up to 120× lower than previous approaches\nL23@P0: based on succinct arguments of knowledge (SNARKs), and\nL24@P0: moreover we avoid the need for query-dependent pre-processing\nL25@P0: which is required by optimized SNARK-based schemes. In our\nL26@P0: construction, the server/client time and the communication cost\nL27@P0: are comparable to, and sometimes smaller than, those of existing\nL28@P0: customized solutions which only support specific queries.\nL29@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL30@P0: All major cloud providers offer Database-as-a-Service solu\u0002tions that allow companies and individuals (clients) to alleviate\nL31@P0: storage costs and achieve resource elasticity by delegating\nL32@P0: storage and maintenance of their data to a cloud server.\nL33@P0: A client can then query and/or update its data using, e.g.,\nL34@P0: standard SQL queries. Outsourcing data in this way, however,\nL35@P0: introduces new security challenges: in particular, the client\nL36@P0: may need to ensure the integrity of the results returned by the\nL37@P0: server. Providing such a guarantee is important if the client\nL38@P0: does not trust the server, or even if the client is concerned\nL39@P0: about the possibility of server errors or external compromise.\nL40@P0: Prior works1 on verifiable computation/outsourcing and\nL41@P0: authenticated data structures address exactly this problem (see\nL42@P0: Section I-D for a detailed discussion), but have significant\nL43@P0: drawbacks. Generic solutions (e.g., SNARKs) can be used\nL44@P0: to verify arbitrary computations, but impose an unacceptable\nL45@P0: overhead at the server. Function-specific schemes (e.g., authen\u0002ticated data structures) target specific classes of computations\nL46@P0: and can be much more efficient than generic solutions; how\u00021We assume no trusted hardware, nor are we willing to assume multiple,\nL47@P0: non-colluding servers (as required by [22]).\nL48@P0: ever, they suffer from limited expressiveness, and in particular\nL49@P0: they cannot handle a wide range of SQL queries.\nL50@P0: A. Our Results\nL51@P0: In this work we present vSQL, a system for verifiable SQL\nL52@P0: queries. vSQL allows a client who owns a relational database\nL53@P0: to outsource it to an untrusted server while storing only a small\nL54@P0: digest locally. Later, the client can issue arbitrary SQL queries\nL55@P0: to the server, who returns the query’s result. (In the case of\nL56@P0: an update query, the result is an updated digest.) The client\nL57@P0: can then verify the validity of the result using an interactive\nL58@P0: protocol with the server; if the result returned by the server is\nL59@P0: incorrect, the client will reject with overwhelming probability.\nL60@P0: vSQL overcomes the drawbacks of existing works. It is\nL61@P0: highly expressive, supporting any computation expressed as\nL62@P0: an arithmetic circuit (which in particular means arbitrary\nL63@P0: SQL queries, including updates) efficiently. We empirically\nL64@P0: demonstrate vSQL’s concrete performance and expressiveness\nL65@P0: using the TPC-H [7] benchmark, and find that the server-side\nL66@P0: computation (which is usually the limiting factor in verifiable\u0002computation schemes) is 5–120× better for vSQL than it is in\nL67@P0: highly optimized libsnark-based constructions [4] (that further\nL68@P0: require query-dependent preprocessing), and comparable to or\nL69@P0: better than a state-of-the-art database-delegation scheme [60]\nL70@P0: that only supports a limited subset of SQL.\nL71@P0: At a high level, vSQL gains efficiency by combining\nL72@P0: two different approaches. First, vSQL uses a highly efficient\nL73@P0: information-theoretic interactive proof system [25] for delegat\u0002ing computations expressed as arithmetic circuits. This reduces\nL74@P0: the number of cryptographic operations performed by the\nL75@P0: server from linear in the circuit size to linear in the lengths of\nL76@P0: the circuit’s inputs and outputs. Second, vSQL supports non\u0002deterministic computation by allowing the server to provide\nL77@P0: auxiliary inputs to the circuit. We achieve this by using a novel\nL78@P0: scheme for verifiable polynomial delegation with knowledge\u0002extraction properties, improving previous results [56]. To the\nL79@P0: best of our knowledge, our protocol is the first implementation\nL80@P0: of an argument system that supports general non-deterministic\nL81@P0: computations and simultaneously achieves the following two\nL82@P0: properties: (1) the number of cryptographic operations per\u0002formed by the prover depends linearly on the circuit’s input\nL83@P0: and output size, and (2) the total prover time is quasilinear in\nL84@P0: the size of the evaluated circuit. In addition, vSQL benefits\nL85@P0: from several performance optimizations that improve both the\nL86@P0: server’s and client’s concrete efficiency (see Section VI). We\nL87@P0-1: describe our techniques in further detail next.B. Our Techniques\nL88@P1: Reducing Cryptographic Overhead. A key building block\nL89@P1: of vSQL is the information-theoretic interactive proof system\nL90@P1: due to Cormode et al. [25] (the CMT protocol) that allows\nL91@P1: a client to verify that y = C(x) for some circuit C and\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVSQL: Verifying Arbitrary SQL Queries over Dynamic Outsourced Databases - Illinois Experts (https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/vsql-verifying-arbitrary-sql-queries-over-dynamic-outsourced-data/)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/vsql-verifying-arbitrary-sql-queries-over-dynamic-outsourced-data/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 191\nL0: 【0†Skip to main navigation】 【1†Skip to search】 【2†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【3†Illinois Experts Home】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【4†LOGIN & Help   Link opens in a new tab†go.illinois.edu】\nL5: \nL6: Search content at Illinois Experts [Input: Search]\nL7: \nL8: # VSQL: Verifying Arbitrary SQL Queries over Dynamic Outsourced Databases\nL9: \nL10:   * 【5†Yupeng Zhang】\nL11:   * , Daniel Genkin\nL12:   * , Jonathan Katz\nL13:   * , Dimitrios Papadopoulos\nL14:   * , Charalampos Papamanthou\nL15: \nL16: Research output: Chapter in Book/Report/Conference proceeding › Conference contribution\nL17: ## Abstract\nL18: Cloud database systems such as Amazon RDS or Google Cloud SQLenable the outsourcing of a large database to a server who then responds to SQL queries. A natural problem here is to efficiently verify the correctness of responses returned by the (untrusted) server. In this paper we present vSQL, a novel cryptographic protocol for publicly verifiable SQL queries on dynamic databases.\nL19: At a high level, our construction relies on two extensions of the CMT interactive-proof protocol [Cormode et al., 2012]: (i) supporting outsourced input via the use of a polynomial-delegation protocol with succinct proofs, and (ii) supporting auxiliary input (i.e., non-deterministic computation) efficiently.\nL20: Compared to previous verifiable-computation systems based on interactive proofs, our construction has verification cost polylogarithmic in the auxiliary input (which for SQL queries can be as large as the database) rather than linear. In order to evaluate the performance and expressiveness of our scheme, we tested it on SQL queries based on the TPC-H benchmark on a database with 6 million rows and 13 columns.\nL21: The server overhead in our scheme (which is typically the main bottleneck) is up to 120 times lower than previousapproaches based on succinct arguments of knowledge (SNARKs), and moreover we avoid the need for query-dependent pre-processing which is required by optimized SNARK-based schemes. In our construction, the server/client time and the communication cost are comparable to, and sometimessmaller than, those of existing customized solutions which only support specific queries.\nL22: Original language  | English (US)\nL23: Title of host publication  | 2017 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, SP 2017 - Proceedings\nL24: Publisher  | 【6†Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.】\nL25: Pages  | 863-880\nL26: Number of pages  | 18\nL27: ISBN (Electronic)  | 9781509055326\nL28: DOIs  |\nL29:   * 【7†https://doi.org/10.1109/SP.2017.43†doi.org】\nL30: State  | Published - Jun 23 2017\nL31: Externally published  | Yes\nL32: Event  | 【6†2017 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, SP 2017】 - San Jose, United States\nL33: Duration: May 22 2017 → May 24 2017\nL34: ### Publication series\nL35: \nL36: Name  | Proceedings - IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy\nL37: ISSN (Print)  | 1081-6011\nL38: \nL39: ### Other\nL40: \nL41: Other  | 2017 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, SP 2017\nL42: Country/Territory  | United States\nL43: City  | San Jose\nL44: Period  | 5/22/17 → 5/24/17\nL45: \nL46: ## ASJC Scopus subject areas\nL47: \nL48:   * Safety, Risk, Reliability and Quality\nL49:   * Software\nL50:   * Computer Networks and Communications\nL51: \nL52: ## Online availability\nL53: \nL54:   * 【7†10.1109/SP.2017.43†doi.org】\nL55: ## Library availability\nL56: \nL57: 【8†Discover UIUC Full Text†i-share-uiu.primo.exlibrisgroup.com】\nL58: \nL59: ## Related links\nL60: \nL61:   * 【9†Link to publication in Scopus†www.scopus.com】\nL62: \nL63:   * 【10†Link to the citations in Scopus†www.scopus.com】\nL64: \nL65: ## Fingerprint\nL66: \nL67: Dive into the research topics of 'VSQL: Verifying Arbitrary SQL Queries over Dynamic Outsourced Databases'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\nL68: \nL69: 【11†View full fingerprint】\nL70: ## Cite this\nL71: \nL72:   * APA\nL73:   * Standard\nL74:   * Harvard\nL75:   * Vancouver\nL76:   * Author\nL77:   * BIBTEX\nL78:   * RIS\nL79: \nL80: 【5†Zhang, Y.】, Genkin, D., Katz, J., Papadopoul